---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { absoluteUrl, seo } from '../../data/seo';

const meta = seo.conformance;
const siteUrl = Astro.site?.toString() || 'https://tensor-website.pages.dev/';
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  headline: meta.title,
  description: meta.description,
  url: absoluteUrl(siteUrl, meta.canonicalPath),
};
---

<BaseLayout title={meta.title} description={meta.description} canonicalPath={meta.canonicalPath} ogType={meta.ogType} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="container prose-card">
    <p class="section-kicker">Evidence before a compatibility claim</p>
    <h2>Reference validation is the first step</h2>
    <p>Core 0.1.0-draft.1 includes schemas, two synthetic cases, a reference validator, and executable tests. They exercise a candidate binding. They are not a certification program or proof of independent interoperability.</p>
    <p><a href="/core/0.1.0-draft.1/coverage.md">Read the exact validation coverage and limitations</a> before describing an implementation as compatible.</p>

    <h2>Run the reference checks</h2>
    <p>From the <code>codex/core-investigation-contract</code> review branch with Node.js 22 or later and dependencies installed:</p>
    <pre><code>{`npm run check:schemas
npm run validate:examples
npm test`}</code></pre>
    <p><a href="/implement/">Full setup instructions</a> · <a href="/investigation-lab/">Validate a bundle in the Lab</a></p>

    <h2>What the checks can establish</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Structural validity against the candidate binding and schemas.</li>
      <li>Definition pins, reference closure, question context, and explicit transition selections.</li>
      <li>Revision lineage, original inputs, and required review representation.</li>
      <li>Declared handling of partial exchange and unsupported required semantics.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>They do not establish evidence truth, legal authority, investigative completeness, security effectiveness, or the safety of an implementation. The validator does not execute recorded actions or fetch evidence locators.</p>

    <h2>Roles replace a universal badge</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Proposed role</th><th>Claim to substantiate</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Definition producer</td><td>Produces pinned, interpretable definitions with explicit question and transition meaning.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Record producer</td><td>Produces records with valid context, attribution, references, and revision behavior.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Reader</td><td>Interprets supported content and exposes missing or unsupported meaning.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Lossless exchanger</td><td>Preserves meaning, required original bytes, and unsupported optional content as opaque data through exchange.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Transition interpreter</td><td>Resolves explicitly selected assessments and guarded transitions without inventing a default.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Report a result others can reproduce</h2>
    <p>Record the specification and binding versions, implementation version, role, profile, test suite and artifact digests, test date, named checks, outcomes, and known exclusions. Distinguish <code>pass</code>, <code>fail</code>, <code>indeterminate</code>, and <code>notRun</code>. An unknown investigative answer can be valid data.</p>
    <p>The founding package supplies <a href="/founding/04-reference-cases-and-conformance.md">twelve acceptance scenarios</a>. They describe desired evidence and are broader than a single reference implementation. A self round trip is not an exchange between independent implementations.</p>

    <h2>Earlier fixtures</h2>
    <p>The previous website's graph-only starter fixtures belong to the legacy model. Use <a href="/core/">the candidate release page</a> for new work; do not combine the two contracts under one conformance claim.</p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
